<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nasir al-Din al-Tusi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1201">1201</a>–<a href="/years/1274">1274</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ṭūsī, Naṣīr al-Dīn al-Ṭūsī, Tusi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nasir al-Din al-Tusi was a Persian polymath, philosopher, scientist and Shi'ite scholar, born in Tus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>. He worked under an Ismāʿīlī ruler at Alamūt, and when Hūlāgū Khān and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> captured Alamūt in 1256 he entered Hūlāgū's service as an astrologer and helped save numerous libraries from destruction. From 1259 an observatory was built to the west of Maragheh, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> Empire. He compiled the astronomical tables known as the Zīj al-Khānī, devised the Ṭūsī couple, criticised Ptolemaic astronomy and is often credited with making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trigonometry">trigonometry</a> a discipline of its own. His works include The Nasirean Ethics of 1235.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Al-Tusi was born in Tus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, into a Shi'a family, and lost his father young. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> and Tus he studied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jafari-school">Ja'fari</a> jurisprudence, logic, philosophy, mathematics, medicine and astronomy. He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> to study philosophy under Farid al-Din Damad and mathematics under Muhammad Hasib, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> studied mathematics and astronomy with Kamal al-Din ibn Yunus. As the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> swept his homeland he entered the service of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismaili-state">Nizari Ismaili state</a>, moving from stronghold to stronghold. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quhistan">Quhistan</a> he wrote the Nasirean Ethics in 1235, and he was later sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> and Maymun-Diz under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizari</a> imam Ala al-Din Muhammad.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He was captured when Maymun-Diz capitulated to the Mongol forces of Hulegu Khan, and in 1256 was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> when it was attacked. Some sources claim that he betrayed its defences. Hulegu, interested in the natural sciences, treated him with great respect, made him scientific adviser and a member of his inner council. It is widely assumed that he was with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> when they attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and massacred its inhabitants in 1258, a point of controversy. Afterwards he was given charge of religious endowments and travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Shi'i institutions, and is credited with helping to preserve foundations and libraries.</p><p>He persuaded Hulegu to build an observatory, begun in 1259 at Maragheh, the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>. From its observations he compiled the Zij-i Ilkhani, completed in 1272. His planetary models used a geometrical device, the Tusi couple, which produces linear motion from two circular motions and replaced Ptolemy's equant for many planets. He calculated annual precession of the equinoxes as 51 arcseconds. His Treatise on the Quadrilateral set out spherical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trigonometry">trigonometry</a> apart from astronomy, and he has about 150 works, 25 of them in Persian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Tajrid al-I'tiqad, a work of Shi'ite theology, has attracted over 400 commentaries, and the Nasirean Ethics, which compares Islamic teaching with the ethics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>, was popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Historian F. Jamil Ragep calls the Zij-i Ilkhani not particularly original, but holds that his planetary theory had enormous influence on late medieval Islamic astronomy. Scholars debate whether the Tusi couple reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a>; there is no evidence that Tusi's own work did, and the couple could have been rederived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclid">Euclid</a>. A lunar crater, the minor planet 10269 Tusi and the K. N. Toosi University of Technology bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nasir al-Din al-Tusi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
